1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
Dad, hotel, motel, youth hostel. What's the difference?

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,700
Easy! Pricey is a hotel, cheap is a motel, cheaper still is a youth hostel!

3
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:11,833
Sir, those names have nothing to do with price.

4
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:12,233
…Huh?

5
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:16,033
Wrong answer number one. I'm Modapman.

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:23,400
Case summary. Family trip, day one. Location: a youth hostel front desk.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,300
Wait, isn't this place for students only? Are we getting kicked out?

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,200
There's no age limit here. We have family rooms too.

9
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,667
Wrong answer, two. Mom, the model answer… usually.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,967
Then why is it called a "mo"-tel?

11
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:41,067
That's… because it's the Mo family's hotel! Mo-tel! It's ours!

12
00:00:41,067 --> 00:00:42,033
No.

13
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:46,933
Wrong answer, three. The Mo family's hotel empire: cancelled.

14
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:49,900
Before check-in, let's do just three rounds.

15
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:51,500
Check-in has rounds…?

16
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:56,600
Round one. Hotel, hostel… and hospital.

17
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,600
All three come from a Latin word for a house that takes in guests.

18
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:01,933
The hospital too?

19
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:06,233
Long ago, it was a house that took in travelers and the sick.

20
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:08,367
Hospital equals hotel. Noted.

21
00:01:08,367 --> 00:01:10,700
Noted. Incorrectly.

22
00:01:10,733 --> 00:01:15,167
Round two. The "mo" in motel is motor. As in, car.

23
00:01:15,167 --> 00:01:20,633
1925, America. A place opened where you park at your door and go straight to bed.

24
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:25,433
Motor hotel. Legend says the full name didn't fit on the sign, so they shortened it.

25
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:28,367
Not Mo… but motor.

26
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:31,600
Then we can go to a motel too! We have a car!

27
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:33,833
Uh… well…

28
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:35,700
We booked here.

29
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:42,733
Round three. Germany, 1909. A teacher was hiking with kids when a storm hit.

30
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:49,933
They barely got shelter in an empty school. So in 1912, he set up a kids' lodge inside a castle.

31
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:51,267
They slept in a castle?!

32
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:55,367
That was the first youth hostel. A home for kids traveling on foot.

33
00:01:55,367 --> 00:02:01,567
Korean law splits them too. Hotels are tourist lodging; youth hostels are youth activity facilities.

34
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:06,033
And motels? The law doesn't even use the word. It's just "general lodging."

35
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:08,300
No official name, yet the biggest sign…

36
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:15,267
In one line: hotels for guests, motels for cars, youth hostels for kids on foot.

37
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:17,867
It's not about price. It's about who the house is for.

38
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:21,000
A hotel is a house that welcomes guests from far away.

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,833
A motel is where people who've driven a long way can rest right away.

40
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:30,733
And a youth hostel is a house a teacher made for kids like you who travel on foot.

41
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:32,567
Then this one's the most ours.

42
00:02:32,567 --> 00:02:35,000
Yep. A house made just for you.

43
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:38,133
My kid took the room key with both hands.

44
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,733
Okay, I'll park right by our door, yes? Like a motel!

45
00:02:41,733 --> 00:02:44,833
The parking lot's at the bottom of the hill. You walk up.

46
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:46,167
…Oh.

47
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,167
Three suitcases. One very steep hill.

48
00:02:49,167 --> 00:02:52,733
Dad, it's a walking trip! Just like that teacher!

49
00:02:52,733 --> 00:02:55,700
Wrong answer, four. Modapman strikes again.
